/*Domain model first
Start with Model.entety (Patient, ClinicalData, Bed, Room, Department, plus enums).
If you don’t fully own these objects, algorithm/persistence logic will stay fuzzy.

State container
Learn Algorithm.AssignmentState next.
This is the “truth table” of who is in which bed; almost every layer depends on it.

Hard constraints before optimization
Read Algorithm.feasibility.HardConstraints and FeasibilityChecker.
This defines legal vs illegal placements (clinical fit, cohorting, ventilator, negative pressure, etc.).

Cost function stack
Then read:

Algorithm.CostCalculator
Algorithm.cost.* (Safety, Policy, Transfer, Unassigned, Clinical)
This explains what “better assignment” mathematically means.
Search mechanics
Then:

Algorithm.greedy.GreedyWarmStart
Algorithm.neighborhood.*
Algorithm.sa.SimulatedAnnealingEngine
This gives you “how candidate states are generated and accepted.”
Controller orchestration
Controller.DefaultAssignmentWorkflowService
This is the pipeline: feasibility -> greedy -> SA -> proposal/preview -> approve/reject/admit/discharge.

Persistence pipeline
Read in this order:

Persistence.dto.* + WardStateDocument
WardStateMapper
JsonFileWardStateRepository
AtomicJsonFiles, PersistenceJson, PersistencePaths
This explains in-memory graph <-> JSON snapshot <-> disk.
UI shell last
View.OptiCareApp + WardDialogs + OptiCareViewFactory
This is easier once you already know model/controller/persistence*/
